  "Question": "If \\(n\\) and \\(m\\) are integers and \\(n^2 + m^2\\) is even, which of the following is impossible?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(n\\) and \\(m\\) are even",
    "B": "\\(n\\) and \\(m\\) are odd",
    "C": "\\(n + m\\) is even",
    "D": "\\(n + m\\) is odd",
    "E": "none of these are impossible"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Squaring preserves whether an integer is odd or even: an even integer times itself is even, while an odd integer times itself is odd.\n\nFor \\(n^2+m^2\\) to be even, the two squares must either both be even or both be odd. Consequently, \\(n\\) and \\(m\\) must either both be even or both be odd. In both cases, \\(n+m\\) is even.\n\nBoth possibilities actually occur: \\(n=m=2\\) satisfies the condition with two even integers, and \\(n=m=1\\) satisfies it with two odd integers. Thus A, B, and C are possible, but \\(n+m\\) cannot be odd. The answer is choice D.",
